Build the locations database without PyGObject (OHOS: _gi cannot load)

libgweather's location database is a GVariant blob generated at build
time by build-aux/meson/gen_locations_variant.py, whose only non-stdlib
import is "from gi.repository import GLib".  On OpenHarmony PC that
import can never succeed: the packaged PyGObject 3.58.0 ships
_gi.cpython-312-aarch64-linux-ohos.so, but loading it aborts in the
dynamic linker with

    ImportError: Error relocating .../gi/_gi.cpython-312-aarch64-linux-ohos.so:
                 PyType_Ready: symbol not found

so the data/Locations.bin custom target fails and ninja stops.  This is
a target ABI mismatch, not a missing package, so it cannot be fixed by
requiring pygobject or by passing a different python.

Earlier revisions of this patch pointed the generator at a ctypes shim
that dlopen()ed libglib-2.0.  That works on the device and in a local
build, but it is load-bearing on a dlopen the build environment does not
control: on the CI worker the loader refuses the unsigned conan glib
copies with "Permission denied", the shim's candidate list runs out and
the target fails with

    FAILED: [code:1] data/Locations.bin

so a build that works on the device cannot be reproduced on CI.

The generator is instead pointed at tools/gvariant_pure.py, which
serialises the GVariant normal form itself using only the standard
library: no PyGObject, no ctypes, no libglib at build time.  Its layout
rules were derived from, and its self-test vectors are taken from, real
GLib output (g_variant_get_normal_form on glib 2.88.1), covering the 18
struct shapes, the variable-size and fixed-size arrays and the scalars
that gen_locations_variant.py emits.  The generated bytes are therefore
identical to what PyGObject would have produced.

The directory is taken from the GW_GVARIANT_DIR environment variable the
recipe sets, falling back to the generator's own directory (Conan copies
both the generator and the serialiser side by side into the exported
sources).  tools/gvariant_ctypes.py is kept as a cross-check: setting
GW_GVARIANT_BACKEND=ctypes switches to it, which must produce the same
bytes.  On a target where PyGObject works, drop this patch and the
upstream generator is used unchanged.

--- a/build-aux/meson/gen_locations_variant.py
+++ b/build-aux/meson/gen_locations_variant.py
@@ -1,10 +1,34 @@
 #!/usr/bin/env python3
 
+import os
 import sys
 import math
 import struct
 import xml.etree.ElementTree as ET
-from gi.repository import GLib
+
+# On OpenHarmony PC the PyGObject extension module cannot be loaded at
+# all: _gi.cpython-*-aarch64-linux-ohos.so dies in the dynamic linker
+# with "PyType_Ready: symbol not found", so "from gi.repository import
+# GLib" is not fixable by installing a package.
+#
+# tools/gvariant_pure.py serialises the GVariant normal form with the
+# standard library alone, so the build needs no GLib at all.  That
+# matters beyond PyGObject: the ctypes shim used here previously
+# dlopen()ed libglib, and on the CI worker every conan copy of glib is
+# refused by the loader ("Permission denied"), so the build passed on
+# the device and failed in CI.  Choosing the backend at run time keeps
+# the shim available as a byte-for-byte cross-check.
+sys.path.insert(0, os.environ.get('GW_GVARIANT_DIR') or
+                 os.path.dirname(os.path.abspath(__file__)))
+_backend = os.environ.get('GW_GVARIANT_BACKEND', 'pure')
+if _backend == 'ctypes':
+    import gvariant_ctypes as _gvariant
+else:
+    import gvariant_pure as _gvariant
+
+class GLib(object):
+    """Namespace exposing just the GLib.Variant this generator uses."""
+    Variant = _gvariant.Variant
 
 tree = ET.parse(sys.argv[1])
 root = tree.getroot()
